mkfs.erofs: lay out a tar stream's file data in stream order (unkernel)

The distro images are read over HTTP: the SDK fetches the first `prefetch`
bytes of an image in one request, and anything else lazily, a Range request
per miss (guest/lib/image.sh). That prefix covers a boot only if everything
a boot reads sits at the front. Upstream mkfs.erofs 1.9.4 writes compressed
data breadth-first through the tree, sorted by name, whatever the tar order
(--sort=none applies to uncompressed images only), places the packed inode
of -Efragments last, and inode metadata and directories between the data
or, with --MZ, in a metadata zone after all of it. Three options make the
layout follow an order the caller gives (in the tar stream):

  --sort=tar        files with data are written in tar stream order,
                    compressed or not: the tree walk handles them first, in
                    that order (lib/inode.c), and then the rest as before
                    (directories, symlinks, devices), skipping them
  --nofrag-first=N  the first N of those keep their tails in their own
                    pclusters instead of the packed inode (-Efragments), so
                    their data is complete where it lies
  --MZ-front=N      with --MZ, the metadata zone (every inode and directory
                    block) goes into N blocks reserved right after the
                    superblock instead of after all data; if N is too few,
                    mkfs fails and says how many it needs (the layout does
                    not depend on N, so a second run with that many fits)

Same license as the files it changes: GPL-2.0+ OR MIT (lib/, include/),
MIT (mkfs/main.c).

